</mj-text>
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    EU presidency tested
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What’s happening?</strong> Cyprus has been forced to <a href="https://www.financialgazette.co.uk/article/2026/03/iran-war-challenges-cyprus-time-in-the-eu-hot-seat" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">reschedule several major EU meetings</a> after Iranian drones targeted the island during escalating regional tensions. The attacks came as Cyprus holds the rotating presidency of the Council of the EU. Meetings involving EU ministers on the bloc’s long-term budget and the EU accession paths of Ukraine and Moldova were postponed, along with cybersecurity and health conferences.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Dilemma: </strong>As the EU member closest to the Middle East, Cyprus risks being drawn into the regional conflict after Iranian threats and drone activity near British bases. At the same time, it must continue leading the EU Council presidency, coordinating legislation and ministerial meetings while managing rising security risks at home.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Support:</strong> Greece has deployed warships and fighter jets to reinforce regional security, while France has sent naval frigates and pledged additional air-defense systems to help protect strategic sites. EU institutions are also coordinating closely with Cypriot authorities to ensure the Council presidency can continue functioning despite disruptions. EU ambassadors and crisis teams are monitoring the conflict and preparing possible evacuations of EU citizens.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
