<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 0.5px 1px 1px" border-radius="5px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="5px" padding="0px">
    
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    Senate paves the way
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Summary: Senate blocks effort to limit Trump’s war powers as the Iran conflict escalates. </strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> The US Senate <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yjj28jjd0o" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">rejected a bipartisan resolution</a> that sought to limit President Trump’s authority to continue military strikes against Iran without congressional approval. The proposal aimed to require Congress to authorise further military measures and potentially halt current hostilities. Just two senators crossed party lines: Republican Rand Paul supported the resolution, while Democrat John Fetterman opposed it.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Why? </strong>Most Republicans rejected the resolution, arguing the president already has authority as commander-in-chief to conduct military operations without prior congressional approval during an active conflict. Many lawmakers also view Iran as a major national security threat and worry that restricting Trump’s powers could weaken the US military response. Some Republicans said they may reconsider if the war expands.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Big picture: </strong>The dispute reflects wider concerns in Washington about “forever wars” in the Middle East and the limits of presidential power during conflicts. At the same time, the Senate vote shows strong political support for confronting Iran despite these concerns. If the conflict expands or lasts longer than expected, pressure may grow in Congress to reassert its authority.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
    </mj-section>
    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-section
      background-color="#eff1f4"
