          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="white"
        >
          <h2
            style="
              padding-bottom: 8px;
              color: #06266d;
              text-align: left;
              border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d;
              font-size: 26px;
              line-height: 1.2;
              font-weight: 300;
            "
          >
            Analysis
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          padding="10px 12px"
          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="#000000"
        >
          <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
  Russia holds back
</h2>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Summary: Russia backs Iran politically but avoids military involvement, prioritizing the Ukraine war and strategic interests.</strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>What happened? </strong>Russia has strongly condemned the US-Israeli strikes on Iran but has <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-why-is-russia-not-coming-to-tehrans-aid/a-76214840" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">stopped short of providing direct military support</a> to Tehran. Despite being one of Iran’s closest partners, cooperating on projects like the North-South transport corridor and military cooperation, such as drone technology used in the Ukraine war, Moscow is unlikely to intervene. Russia is also balancing its own priorities, including the war in Ukraine and complex relations with Israel.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Background:</strong> Russia and Iran have developed closer ties over the past decade, largely driven by shared tensions with the West and international sanctions. Their cooperation deepened after Russia’s 2022 invasion of Ukraine, when Iran supplied Shahed drones that significantly boosted Moscow’s battlefield capabilities. In return, Russia has provided Iran with military technology, intelligence support, and diplomatic backing at the UN.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Future:</strong> If the current regime weakens or falls, Moscow may shift quickly to engage any successor government to protect its strategic interests. Russia is likely to continue offering diplomatic backing but prevent direct military involvement to prevent escalation with the US and Israel. At the same time, Moscow may benefit from higher energy prices and global attention shifting away from Ukraine.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
