          >
            Foundations
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
    What is game theory?
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border="1px solid #00000013"
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>

<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What is it?</strong> Game theory analyzes strategic situations in which <a href="https://www.britannica.com/science/game-theory" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">one player's outcome depends on others' choices</a>. It was formalized in the 1940s by the mathematician John von Neumann and the economist Oskar Morgenstern, and became an important tool during the Cold War for modeling nuclear deterrence (convincing an enemy not to attack by threatening a strong response). It is currently widely used in politics, economics, and military planning.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Example</strong>: In a potential Iran-Israel nuclear standoff, Iran must decide whether to continue its nuclear program, knowing Israel might attack. Similarly, Israel would need to consider whether to strike, aware that Iran could retaliate. This is called a “game” because it’s a situation of strategic independence: each side’s best choice depends on what it thinks the other side will do.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Application:</strong> In international relations, game theory can show the sequence of moves and decisions (extensive model), compare all possible strategies and preferred results (strategic model), or measure the value each partner brings to an alliance (coalition model). It provides a clear framework through different scenarios and consequences. While it doesn’t provide a definitive answer, it helps decision makers understand how their choices and assumptions about others’ choices can shape the final outcome.</p>
</mj-text>
